Drive more than one decoder device

This driver opened one video node and one media device and kept the state of
that one queue pair in its globals.  That is the whole of most boards.  It is
not the whole of the H713: the Allwinner video engine (cedrus -- MPEG-2, H.264,
HEVC, VP8, VP9) is /dev/video0, and the AV1 decoder is a different core on a
different node with its own media device.  Nothing here could reach it.

Every stateless decoder on the system is now found at init: the primary one as
before (LIBVA_V4L2_REQUEST_VIDEO_PATH / _MEDIA_PATH, or video0/media0), then
any other streaming video node that offers a coded format this driver has a
backend for and has a media device to allocate requests from.  The media
device is paired with its video node through sysfs, where the two are siblings
under one parent -- the node numbers carry no such promise.

A config picks the first device that offers its coded format, so the primary
keeps everything it can do, and a profile is advertised if any device decodes
it.

The state of a device's queues -- fds, capture format, the format programmed
on the OUTPUT queue -- moves into struct request_device, one per device.  The
existing code is not rewritten to index it: request_use_device() swaps a
device's state into the fields that code already reads, and each entry point
calls it once with the device of the object it was handed (config, context or
surface; every one of them now records its device).  Surfaces are created with
no config or context in sight, so they go to the device of the most recent
config, the same way they already learn the coded pixel format.

The two places that release a device's queues "when nothing refers to them any
more" now ask that question per device.  Asking it globally would either free
the buffers of a decoder that is still in use or never free those of one that
is not, as soon as two had been used in one process -- which is exactly what a
player does when the next file in a playlist is a different codec and the
frames of the last one are still on screen.

